The proposed methodology follows a systematic optimization process.

Step 1: Network Initialization
The IEEE distribution system data, including bus information, branch parameters, switch status, and operational constraints, are provided as input.

Step 2: Initial Population Generation
The Barber Optimization Algorithm randomly generates multiple feasible switch configurations while ensuring that each network remains radial.

Step 3: Load Flow Analysis
Each candidate solution is evaluated using load flow analysis to determine:
•	Total real power loss 
•	Bus voltage magnitude 
•	Voltage deviation 

Step 4: Fitness Evaluation
A weighted multi-objective fitness function is calculated by combining:
•	Real power loss 
•	Voltage deviation 
Lower fitness values indicate better solutions.

Step 5: Barber Optimization Process
BOA improves candidate solutions through exploration and exploitation mechanisms inspired by barber-customer interactions.
The algorithm continuously updates switch configurations to search for the global optimum.

Step 6: Constraint Verification
Each updated solution is checked against operational constraints:
•	Radiality 
•	Bus voltage limits 
•	Branch current limits 
•	Power balance 
Invalid solutions receive penalty values.

Step 7: Convergence
The optimization process continues until the maximum number of iterations is reached or the improvement becomes negligible.

Step 8: Final Solution
The algorithm returns the optimal switch configuration with minimum power loss and improved voltage profile.
